  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current optimization method uses Adam, which excels at local optimization but may get stuck in local minima due to the non-convex nature of the problem. The central idea is to replace the soft-max gradient approximation with a more accurate numerical differentiation technique (central differences) and use a line-search method instead of fixed learning rates. This will allow the optimizer to adaptively adjust steps, potentially escaping local minima. Additionally, a more efficient projection step is implemented to maintain feasibility by solving a least-squares problem with box constraints. Bayesian optimization elements are incorporated via acquisition functions (Expected Improvement) to prioritize promising regions, while the temperature schedule in the soft-max weights is adjusted to balance between smooth transitions and exploration.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent the step function h on a grid of N points (even integer N) over [0, 2] with spacing dx = 2/N. The objective is to minimize:\n  C\u2085 = (1/dx) * max_{k} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) \u22c5 (1 - h(x+k)) dx\nThis is evaluated via discrete correlation of h with (1-h). The constraints are:\n  0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \n  \u2211h[i] * dx \u2248 1 (exactly for finite grids by choosing N even and sum_h = N/2).\n\nThe optimization minimizes the maximum correlation by reformulating the soft-max objective with Gaussian process surrogate for weights (temperature \u03c4) and using the scaled correlation:\n  F(h) = ln( max\u2096 <h \u2297 (1-h_\u03ba) |_\u03ba\u2096 + \u03b5 )   [weighted by \u03c4-dependent scaling]\n\nThe algorithm uses projected gradients via central differences for derivatives. The projection step solves a quadratic program for enforcing the constraints:\n  minimize \u2016h - h_proj\u2016\u00b2 \n  s.t. 0 \u2264 h_proj \u2264 1, \u2211h_proj[i] * dx = target_sum\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- The run function uses BayesOpt via a custom acquisition function to select hyperranges for each grid size N (even integers in [46, 92, 380, 572]).\n- For each N, the parent solution (initial_h_values) is interpolated (if N != 380) and passed as an initial guess.\n- Optimization phases (coarse, medium, fine) use line-search Nesterov-style descent with adaptive step sizes, complemented by Bayesian acquisition-driven sampling from the objective landscape.\n- The core gradient computation uses finite differences with clipping to handle numerical stability.\n- The projection step is a constrained least-squares optimizer (using quadratic programming).\n- The algorithm dynamically adjusts temperature \u03c4(t) with a power-law decay and temperature acquisition to prioritize temperature regimes that reduce C\u2085.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Grid size N (from [46, 92, 380, 572]): chosen by Bayesian optimization to spend more time on potentially superior grid sizes.\n- Temperature \u03c4(t): decays as t\u2070.\u2076 with dynamic acquisition-driven restarts.\n- Line-search: uses backtracking with curvature conditions to reduce oscillation.\n- Bayesian exploration: acquisition function dynamically sampled to pick the most promising evaluation point.\n- Projection: uses quadprog.solve_qp for L2-projection onto the nonnegative orthant with fixed sum, then clipped to [0,1].\n- Parallel exploration: multiple grid sizes are interleaved when time permits, implemented via an event-driven scheduling.\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness\n- Primary grid N=380 runs for full optimization time due to its previously good record, but if time limited, proceeds to N=572.\n- Each grid size's execution time T(N) is estimated as O(N\u00b2) due to correlation costs, with O(N) per phase.\n- Phases are dynamically timed to ensure safety: breaks into smaller expansions if deadline passes 80%.\n- After each phase or restart, the best solution is stored and reevaluated to avoid optimizer drift.\n- Handling invalid h (e.g., due to floating-point issues) via automatic re-projection.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- The returned h_values are projected via the L2-constraint method to ensure \u2211h[i] = N/2 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641.\n- Validation includes:\n  a) Clipping any h[i] values outside [0,1].\n  b) Re-evaluating the objective using correlation to ensure the candidate is comparable.\n  c) Computing C\u2085 via `compute_c5` helper to verify against persistence.\n- The objective is strictly the correlation max, scaled by dx.\n- Sanitization steps:\n  - Check if time is exceeded: return best valid candidate.\n  - Check if initial solution violates constraints: project once.\n\nFinal output format:\n- h_values: numpy array of float32, with values between 0 and 1.\n- c5_bound: float (the computed C\u2085 score).\n- n_points: integer number of grid points (even).",
